{"text": " This tiny ball can hit harder than this giant one. It sounds wrong, isn't it? But watch this closely. The big ball is heavier, and so it barely moves. But this tiny ball? It's moving ridiculously fast, and when they collide, the tiny ball can have just as much momentum as the giant one. It sounds crazier when you do this. Double the tiny ball's speed, and its momentum doubles. Try to double it again. And now that little ball is carrying four times the momentum it had at the beginning. So, what actually determines momentum? It's two things, mass and speed. But there's something even more important. When these two objects collide, the momentum doesn't just vanish, it rather moves. One slows down, while the other speeds up. And the total momentum stays the same. So, a tiny object moving fast enough can have more momentum than something hundreds of times heavier. And that's why you should never judge an object's momentum by its size. Because that tiny ball might be the dangerous one here.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "seek": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.54, "text": " This tiny ball can hit harder than this giant one.", "tokens": [50363, 770, 7009, 2613, 460, 2277, 7069, 621, 428, 6175, 530, 13, 50493], "temperature": 0.0, "avg_logprob": -0.10118939993264792, "compression_ratio": 1.8144329896907216, "no_speech_prob": 0.009953301399946213, "words": [{"word": " This", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.24, "probability": 0.8024261593818665}, {"word": " tiny", "start": 0.24, "end": 0.48, "probability": 0.9927835464477539}, {"word": " ball", "start": 0.48, "end": 0.7, "probability": 0.994424045085907}, {"word": " can", "start": 0.7, "end": 0.9, "probability": 0.9945384860038757}, {"word": " hit", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.12, "probability": 0.9970638155937195}, {"word": " harder", "start": 1.12, "end": 1.56, "probability": 0.9752923846244812}, {"word": " than", "start": 1.56, "end": 1.86, "probability": 0.9839524626731873}, {"word": " this", "start": 1.86, "end": 2.06, "probability": 0